***

**4. Create two faceted scatterplots to illustrate the relationship of math and science scores: one for private school students and one for public school students. Put math on the horizontal axis of each plot. Use different colors to indicate the SES for each student in the plots. Put a linear regression line on your plots.**

```{r}

# Converting school type and SES to factors.

student_scores$schtyp <- factor(student_scores$schtyp, levels = c(1, 2), labels = c("Public", "Private"))
student_scores$ses <- as.factor(student_scores$ses)

# Creating scatterplots.

ggplot(student_scores, aes(x = math, y = science, color = ses)) +
  geom_point() +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
  facet_wrap(~ schtyp) +
  labs(
    title = "Math vs. Science Scores by School Type and SES",
    x = "Math Score",
    y = "Science Score",
    color = "SES"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

```


***

**5. Create side-by-side boxplots to illustrate the differences in math scores of students from different SES groups. Include points for every student in your plot. Also plot the mean for each group.**

```{r}

# Using ggplot to create boxplots.

ggplot(student_scores, aes(x = ses, y = math)) + 
  geom_boxplot(aes(fill = ses), outlier.colour = "red", outlier.shape = 16, outlier.size = 3) +  
  geom_jitter(aes(color = ses), width = 0.2, alpha = 0.6) +  # Points for each student
  stat_summary(aes(group = ses), fun = mean, geom = "point", shape = 18, size = 4, color = "black") +  # Mean points
  labs(
    title = "Math Scores by SES Group",
    x = "SES Group",
    y = "Math Score",
    fill = "SES",
    color = "SES"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

```


