**7. Use the mental health study data to graphically explore the relationship of life events and SES on mental health impairment. Your graph should illustrate whether SES moderates the influence of life events on mental health.**

```{r}

# Using read_excel since file was Excel.

mental_health_study <- read_excel(here("data", "Mental Health Study Data.xlsx"))

```


```{r}


# Converting SES to numeric.

mental_health_study$SES <- as.numeric(as.character(mental_health_study$SES))

# Recoding SES into a new categorical variable instead of overwriting.

mental_health_study$SES_group <- cut(
  mental_health_study$SES,
  breaks = 3,
  labels = c("Low", "Medium", "High")
)

# Creating the plot using the new SES_group variable.

ggplot(mental_health_study, aes(x = LifeEvts, y = MentImp, color = SES_group)) +
  geom_point(alpha = 0.6) +
  geom_smooth(method = "lm", se = FALSE) +
  labs(
    title = "Interaction of Life Events and SES on Mental Health Impairment",
    x = "Life Events",
    y = "Mental Health Impairment",
    color = "SES"
  ) +
  theme_minimal()

```


